(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endocrine-physiology-3-quiz"]={"title":"Endocrine Physiology III: Reproductive Hormones — Quiz (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Approximately how many spermatozoa does a normal male produce per day?","choices":["120 million","1.2 billion","12 thousand","1.2 million"],"answer":0,"exp":"A normal male produces up to 120 million spermatozoa per day. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why must the testes be kept about 2°C cooler than core body temperature?","choices":["Sperm are fragile, survive only a matter of days at body temperature, and have limited energy capacity, making cooler temperatures protective","Sperm actually require elevated core body heat to mature properly and would rapidly die if kept at lower, cooler temperatures.","There is no meaningful temperature sensitivity at all in sperm production, maturation, or long-term survival.","Cooler scrotal temperature directly increases Leydig cell testosterone production and overall secretion rate."],"answer":0,"exp":"Sperm are fragile with limited energy capacity and only survive a matter of days at body temperature, so keeping the testes cooler than the core helps protect them. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which gland secretes mucoid material containing fructose, citric acid, prostaglandins, and fibrinogen as part of the ejaculate?","choices":["Seminal vesicles","Bulbourethral glands","Epididymis","Prostate gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"The seminal vesicles secrete mucoid material containing fructose, citric acid, prostaglandins, and fibrinogen. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Per the composition of semen described in this lecture, what are the approximate volume contributions of sperm, prostate secretions, and seminal vesicle secretions?","choices":["10% sperm, 30% prostate, 60% seminal vesicles","50% sperm, 25% prostate, 25% seminal vesicles","90% sperm, 5% prostate, 5% seminal vesicles","33% each"],"answer":0,"exp":"Semen is composed of approximately 10% sperm, 30% prostate secretions, and 60% seminal vesicle secretions, with an average pH of 7.5. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is capacitation of spermatozoa?","choices":["Changes occurring after ejaculation that prepare sperm for fertilization","The initial formation of sperm in the seminiferous tubules","The process by which ejaculated semen coagulates and later liquefies","The maturation and storage process occurring within the epididymis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Capacitation refers to changes that occur after ejaculation — including loss of inhibitory factors, cholesterol loss weakening the membrane, and increased calcium permeability — that allow sperm to fertilize an ovum. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"During capacitation, what happens to the sperm's acrosome that enables penetration of the ovum?","choices":["It dissolves completely and is lost before fertilization","It converts to a structure resembling the trophoblast","It becomes completely impermeable to extracellular calcium ions","It contains hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes used for penetration"],"answer":3,"exp":"The acrosome contains hyaluronidase and proteolytic enzymes that enable the sperm to penetrate the ovum. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Luteinizing hormone (LH), from the anterior pituitary, regulates spermatogenesis by stimulating which cells to secrete testosterone?","choices":["Leydig cells","Spermatogonia directly","Sertoli cells","Epididymal cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"LH stimulates Leydig cells to secrete testosterone. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) regulates spermatogenesis by stimulating which cells?","choices":["Sertoli cells","Trophoblast cells","Leydig cells","Chief cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"FSH stimulates Sertoli cells, which provide support for spermatogonia. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Puberty's gradual onset is marked by GnRH beginning around which age?","choices":["The 18th year of life","The moment of birth","The 30th year of life","The 8th year of life"],"answer":3,"exp":"GnRH gradually starts around the 8th year of life, with puberty onset typically occurring between ages 11–16. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Menarche is defined as:","choices":["The permanent cessation of menstrual cycles, with average onset near age 51","The point at which estrogen levels are said to permanently peak for life","The beginning of the cycle of menstruation, with average onset before age 13","The onset of male puberty, typically occurring around age 12"],"answer":2,"exp":"Menarche is the beginning of the cycle of menstruation, with an average age of onset under 13 years. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Menopause, occurring on average around what age, is characterized by which hormonal pattern?","choices":["Average 25 years; high estrogen/progesterone","Average 51.4 years; low estrogen/progesterone with high gonadotropins","Average 51.4 years; high estrogen/progesterone with low gonadotropins","There is no described average age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Menopause occurs on average at 51.4 years (range 40–50), with irregular menstrual cycles, obsolescence of the ovaries, and low estrogen/progesterone alongside (compensatorily) elevated gonadotropins. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Estrogen's effect on the fallopian tube's ciliated epithelium helps accomplish which function?","choices":["Guiding/moving the ovum toward the tube opening (ostium) after it enters the fallopian tube","Preventing ovum transport entirely once it has left the ovarian follicle","Actively destroying and enzymatically breaking down any unfertilized ova present.","Producing progesterone locally within the fallopian tube epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Estrogen stimulates the ciliated epithelium of the fallopian tube's fimbriated ends, which helps move the ovum toward the tube's opening (ostium), with a described 98% success rate. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Estrogen's physiological effects on the uterus include an increase in size by approximately:","choices":["10×","1.5×","2–3×","No change in size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Estrogen increases uterine size by approximately 2–3×, along with proliferation of endometrial stroma and glands. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Estrogen's skeletal effect involves osteoclastic activity regulated via which mechanism?","choices":["No skeletal effect at all","Direct stimulation of osteoclast proliferation with no inhibition","Complete suppression of all bone remodeling","An osteoclastogenic inhibitory factor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Estrogen's skeletal effect on osteoclastic activity operates through an osteoclastogenic inhibitory factor. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Compared to testosterone, estrogen's effect on protein deposition is:","choices":["A net decrease in overall protein deposition throughout the body","A much stronger increase than testosterone's protein effect","Exactly identical in magnitude to testosterone's protein effect","A slight increase, not as strong as testosterone's"],"answer":3,"exp":"Estrogen causes a slight increase in protein deposition, though not as strong an effect as testosterone's. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Progesterone's effect on the endometrium is best described as promoting:","choices":["Complete immediate shedding of the endometrium with no secretory phase","Progressive atrophy of the endometrial lining over the cycle","Secretory changes in the endometrium (the endometrial/menstrual cycle)","No measurable effect on the endometrium at any stage of the cycle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Progesterone promotes secretory changes in the endometrium, driving the endometrial (menstrual) cycle. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Progesterone's effect on the fallopian tubes is to promote secretion by the mucosal lining, which serves what purpose?","choices":["Has no functional purpose","Prevents sperm from entering the fallopian tube","Provides a nutrient source for the ovum","Dissolves the ovum before fertilization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Progesterone-driven mucosal secretion in the fallopian tubes provides a nutrient source for the ovum. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Progesterone's effect on the breasts includes promoting the secretory capability of which structures?","choices":["Adipose tissue deposition","Lobules and alveoli","Ductile system growth only","The nipple-areolar complex exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Progesterone promotes development of lobules and alveoli and their secretory capability, while estrogen contributes more to stromal tissue, ductile system growth, and fat deposition. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which hormone, secreted from granulosa/corpus luteum cells, suppresses GnRH release as part of ovarian feedback?","choices":["Prolactin","ADH","Oxytocin","Inhibin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Inhibin suppresses GnRH release as part of the ovarian feedback system. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Estrogen's effect on vaginal epithelium is to change it from:","choices":["Stratified to cuboidal","No change in epithelial type","Cuboidal to stratified","Simple squamous to transitional"],"answer":2,"exp":"Estrogen changes the vaginal epithelium from cuboidal to stratified. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Estrogen's electrolyte effect is a slight increase in retention of which substances?","choices":["Potassium and calcium","Sodium and water","Chloride only, with no effect on sodium","Phosphate and magnesium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Estrogen has a slight effect to increase sodium and water retention. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Estrogen's effect on skin and hair is described as:","choices":["Little impact on hair, but smooth and vascularized skin","Thickened, toughened skin with heavy sebaceous secretion","Complete loss of skin vascularity","Strong stimulation of hair growth, similar to testosterone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Estrogen has little impact on hair but softens the skin and makes it more vascularized. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Where does fertilization of the ovum typically occur?","choices":["In the lower portion of the cervical canal","In the ampullae of the fallopian tubes","In the ovary itself, before ovulation occurs","In the upper cavity of the uterus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fertilization occurs in the ampullae of the fallopian tubes. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What propels sperm toward the ovum in the fallopian tube, in response to seminal fluid prostaglandins?","choices":["Sperm swimming entirely on their own with no external assistance","Uterine contractions and fallopian tube cilia","Peristalsis of the vas deferens only","Gravity and passive downward fluid flow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sperm are propelled by uterine contractions and fallopian tube cilia, stimulated in response to seminal fluid prostaglandins. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What relaxes the isthmus of the uterine tube, allowing the fertilized ovum to enter the uterus?","choices":["FSH from the anterior pituitary","Estrogen from the follicle","Corpus luteum-derived progesterone","Inhibin from granulosa cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Corpus luteum-derived progesterone relaxes the isthmus of the uterine tube, allowing entry of the ovum into the uterus. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"About how long does it take for the fertilized ovum to travel and enter the uterus as a blastocyst?","choices":["A few hours","2–3 weeks","3–5 days","1–2 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"It takes a total of about 3–5 days for the fertilized ovum to enter the uterus as a blastocyst (about 100 cells). — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Implantation of the fertilized ovum into the endometrium is driven primarily by which cells?","choices":["Parathyroid chief cells","Sertoli cells","Trophoblast cells","Leydig cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Trophoblast cells invade the endometrial lining, envelope the blastocyst, provide nutrients, and later develop into the placenta and membranes of pregnancy. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Trophoblasts differentiate into two layers. Which layer is a multinucleated mass without cellular boundaries, exposed to maternal blood?","choices":["Decidual tissue layer","Cytotrophoblast","Chorion frondosum","Syncytiotrophoblast"],"answer":3,"exp":"The syncytiotrophoblast is the multinucleated mass without cellular boundaries, exposed to maternal blood. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How many umbilical arteries and umbilical veins are described in normal placental circulation, and what does each carry?","choices":["1 artery (oxygenated blood to fetus), 2 veins (deoxygenated blood from fetus)","2 arteries and 2 veins, with all four vessels carrying identically mixed blood","1 artery and 1 vein, with both vessels carrying fully oxygenated blood","2 arteries (deoxygenated from fetus to villi), 1 vein (oxygenated from villi to fetus)"],"answer":3,"exp":"There are 2 umbilical arteries carrying deoxygenated blood from the fetus to the villi, and 1 umbilical vein carrying oxygenated blood from the villi back to the fetus. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What role does progesterone play in preparing for implantation and early pregnancy nutrition?","choices":["It has essentially no meaningful physiological role at all until the onset of parturition itself.","It prepares the uterine lining for implantation, which then provides nutrients to the conceptus","It solely and directly regulates the developing fetal heart rate throughout gestation.","It only affects maternal mood and emotional state, with no direct uterine role."],"answer":1,"exp":"Progesterone prepares the uterine lining for implantation, and that lining then provides nutrients to the conceptus (embryo plus adjacent membranes). — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 64"}]};
